        {
          "id": "SP_THALASSOPTERUS_ZANCLEUS_KWIETNIEWSKI_1910",
          "name": "Thalassopterus zancleus Kwietniewski, 1910",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Thalassopterus",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "<p>The holotype observed by Spengel in 1893, was an adult female missing most of the posterior region of its trunk. It had a yellow proboscis, red collar, and reddish yellow anterior trunk. The holotype was destroyed by 1944 World War II bombings-- therefore, the neotype [Holland et al. 2013] is described below. It must be noted that the neotype described in Holland et al. 2013 was damaged and flattened during collection and fixation.</p> <p>Proboscis and Collar: The proboscis comprises an epidermis including mucous cells, a neurite layer of the intra-epidermal nervous system, and a well-developed basal lamina. A proboscis coelom opens up in a space, in which some of the tissues are projections of a median and the two lateral lobes are of the glomerulus. The extremities of the pericardial vesicle and the stomochord become closely apposed to each other. In this region, a lateral group of glomeruli on either side is associated with the wall of the pericardial vesicle. In contrast to the situation in some other spengelids, no vermiform process is detectable, although such a delicate feature could have been lost.</p> <p> The proboscis skeleton comprises a dense medial region flanked by a mass of chondroid tissue. This striking chondroid tissue comprises a homogeneous extracellular matrix in which run reconnecting channels lined by numerous small cells.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:4359244",
          "authorship": "Kwietniewski",
          "yearPublished": "1910",
          "children": [],
          "uuid": "29fffb46-2fe6-5ea-bed-cafdc081f86bca",
          "sourceIds": {
            "col": [
              "55ZSQ"
            ]
          },
          "authorityIds": "col:55ZSQ"
        },
